{{History of Balisca}}

The "'''Confederal Question'''" ({{Lang-bl|Pregunta confederal}}) was a debate which emerged in the immediate aftermath of the [[Baliscan War of Independence]], over the extent of confederal power in the [[Northern Confederation]]. The Confederal Question encompassed myriad interrelated elements: tax disputes, treaties with [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]], foreign relations and trade, the rise of [[W:Ethnonationalism|ethno]]-[[W:Religious nationalism|religious nationalism]] in its provinces, and the emancipation of [[W:Free people of color|free people of color]].

== Origin and resolution ==
During the [[Baliscan War of Independence]], the rebelling provinces replaced their colonial governments with [[W:Republic|republican]] constitutions based on the principle of [[W:Separation of powers|separation of powers]], organizing government into [[W:Legislative|legislative]], [[W:Executive (government)|executive]], and [[W:Judicial|judicial]] branches. These revolutionary constitutions endorsed [[W:Legislative supremacy|legislative supremacy]] by placing most power in the [[W:Open cabildo|open cabildos]], which were viewed as most representative of the people, including power traditionally considered as belonging to the executive and judicial branches. The [[Granyana Convention]] issued the [[Proclamation of Alcazaba]] in 1808, and the 13 states created a permanent alliance to coordinate their efforts to win the ''Resistencía''. This alliance, the [[Southern Confederation]], was governed according to the [[Articles of Confederation]], which was more of a treaty between independent countries than a national constitution. The Articles were adopted by Cerdanyan and Hesperian delegations simultaneously, but did not include any Bayaran provinces until 1810, and was not fully ratified until after the [[Battle of Navassa]] in 1816.

Under the Articles, the Southern Confederation was a loose union of [[Communities of Balisca|independent republics]], with the Articles guaranteeing state sovereignty and independence. The Confederation was governed by the [[Congress of the Confederation]], a [[W:Unicameral|unicameral]] legislature whose members were chosen by their republic's legislatures and where each province was entitled to one vote. Congress's powers were limited to waging war and directing foreign affairs. It could not levy taxes or tariffs, and was dependent on funds raised by the republics; it could not force delinquent provinces to pay. 

Since the Articles could only be amended by a unanimous vote of the republic's, each had effective [[W:Veto|veto]] power over any proposed change. A [[W:Super majority|super majority]] (sixteen of twenty-three provincial delegations) was required for Congress to pass major legislation such as declaring war, making treaties, or borrowing money. The confederation had a ceremonial [[W:Directorial system|directorial executive]] and no established judiciary, which meant the confederal government lacked effective means to enforce its own laws and treaties against non-compliance. It soon became evident to nearly all that the confederal government, as originally organized, was inadequate for managing the various problems confronting the fledgling confederation.
[[File:Capitao-mato.jpg|left|thumb|334x334px|''Cuarenta acres y una mula'' {{Circa|1823}}, depicting newly emancipated Kafto-Bayaran landowners in [[Macaio Province]]]]
Once the crucial task of winning the war had passed, the republics began to look to their own interests rather than the confederation. During the war, confederal forces had imprisoned, hanged, or expelled all former slave owners (most relocated to Brazil and Argentina), and divided their land and other assets between the newly emancipated [[Kafto-Baliscans|Kafto-Baliscan]] population. This further hardened attitudes in Bayara against confederation, which already was strong due to the devastation in Bayara in terms of infrastructure and economy. Because of the reluctance of the land-owning class accept the loss of their privileges and to grant voting rights to freedmen, the Confederal Congress adopted [[Reconstruction laws]] over Bayaran objections. In response, and intending to avoid the social effects of the war, most of the Bayaran provinces passed [[W:Black codes|black codes]]. In the early years of Confederation, these were mostly ''de jure'' nullified by confederal law and pro-reformist legislatures, but widely continued to be enforced by Bayaran officials.

Increasing calls for the strengthening of confederal power and increasing Hesperian influence in the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]] fueled intercommunal tensions between predominately Volisanian-speaking Cathars and Xacano-speaking Catholics, and the confederal government was increasingly dragged into the position of mediator in the region. Conflict boiled over following the murder of an Catalan [[W:Pastry|pastry]] chef in [[Xàbia]] by a Catholic mob, resulting in [[Pastry War|escalating reprisals and counter-reprisals]] which eventually culminated in [[Confederal emergency|confederal intervention]]. It was ended by the [[Treaty of Navassa]].

The Treaty was largely viewed as illegitimate by Bayarans (who denounced it as unacceptably favorable to the Volisanian) and Estuarians (who were frustrated by it's failure to secure their rights) alike, and became increasingly hard to enforce. It established military districts and governors to rule over the region until new governments could be established. Many Xacano landowners who had actively supported the royalists were temporarily disenfranchised. Further conflict was initially avoided with the establishment of the [[Cerdanyan Legion]] which administered the Estuarian territories. The Legion was withdrawn in 1826 over confederal disputes in regards to the legion's funding. 

Hesperian delegates called for a [[Confederal Convention]] to solve the impending crisis, to be held in [[Alessandria]]. The convention resulted in the [[Compromise of 1827]] which provided for a significantly strengthened central government to handle disputes between the respective republics and their citizens. This was unanimously supported by the delegations of Hesperia and [[Cerdanya]], but was rejected and ultimately ignored by the Bayarans, with the exception of the delegation from [[Macaio]]. These delegates were at the time aware that the republic was well on it's way towards renouncing the [[Articles of Confederation]] and {{W|secession|seceding}} from the Confederation, and sought to pre-empt this by declaring the [[Carpinchera Republic]]. The General Junta denounced the Articles of Confederation on 6 July 1828, and the secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] assumed provisional powers in [[Baliscan Civil War|anticipation of war]].

Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were unable to overcome its geographic disadvantages: it was relatively flat, it was unable to establish control over most high mountain passes across the [[Matarrañya|Matarrañya / Cañadapirén]], and thus were unable to directly threaten [[Hesperia|Hesperian territory]]. Notably however, Bayaran armies crossed the [[Cerroitá Pass]] and attempted to capture the [[Alpujarra]], culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]], which was beaten back following the intervention of the [[Maroon Confederacy]] in support of confederal forces. Bayara suffered substantially more than the rest of the Confederation overall, as the Confederalist strategy of [[W:Attrition warfare|attrition warfare]] meant that Bayaran forces could not replace their casualties, and the total war waged by Hesperian armies devastated the infrastructure and caused widespread poverty and distress.

Bayara was punished severely in the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]]: they were forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the Confederation during the war, in addition to the General Junta being coerced into ratifying the Compromise, '''resulting in the end of the Confederal Question, and marking the beginning of the [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction era]].''' In addition, the Confederal Congress forced the {{W|cede|ceding}} of significant amounts of land in Bayara's north, including [[Trás-as-Montanyas]] (ceded to Hesperia), and the northern wetlands, which was allocated to the now-independent [[Estuaria|Estuarian community]]. 

